From: ricktav <82380971+ricktav@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 08:17:06 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: apple: s800-0-3: Keep the AOP GPIO domain on

The AOP's firmware keeps running while Linux sleeps, but its GPIO
block's power domain, aop_gpio, is not always-on like the AOP's own.
In s2idle genpd turns aop_gpio off in the noirq phase. On an iPhone 6s
Plus (N66) with the AOP started, pm_test=platform then never returned,
and a real s2idle could not be woken by the Power or Home button;
without the AOP driver both worked. Presumably the running firmware
touches the powered-off block and the bus hangs.

Mark aop_gpio always-on, as ps_aop is. With only that changed, on the
same phone with the AOP running, pm_test=platform returns after its
5 s, and s2idle wakes on the Power button, after which the AOP still
answers (the barometer reads).

The file is shared by every S8000 and S8003 board, so this also
changes the iPad 5, which starts the AOP too. It is not tested there.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: ricktav <82380971+ricktav@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/s800-0-3-pmgr.dtsi | 1 +
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/s800-0-3-pmgr.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/s800-0-3-pmgr.dtsi
index 3af0a7093..fc73bfdae 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/s800-0-3-pmgr.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/s800-0-3-pmgr.dtsi
@@ -731,6 +731,7 @@ ps_aop_gpio: power-controller@80010 {
 		#reset-cells = <0>;
 		label = "aop_gpio";
 		power-domains = <&ps_aop>;
+		apple,always-on; /* The running AOP firmware uses its GPIOs */
 	};
 
 	ps_aop_cpu: power-controller@80040 {
